{{block center|<math>e_1 = \text{R}_1 i_1 + \text{SN}_1 \frac{db_1}{dt},\ e_2 = \text{SN}_2 \frac{db_2}{dt} - \text{R}_2 i_2.</math>}}

Hence, if <math>b_1 = b_2</math>, and if <math>\text{R}_1 i_1</math> is negligible in comparison with <math>\text{SN}_1 db/dt</math>, and <math>i = 0</math>, that is, if the secondary circuit is open, then <math>e_1/e_2 = \text{N}_1/\text{N}_2</math>, or the transformation ratio is simply the ratio of the windings. This, however, is not the case if <math>b_1</math> and <math>b_2</math> have not the same value; in other words, if there is magnetic leakage. If the magnetic leakage can be neglected, then the resultant magnetizing force, and therefore the iron core loss, is constant at all loads. Accordingly, the relation between the primary current (<math>i_1</math>), the secondary current (<math>i_2</math>), and the magnetizing current (<math>i</math>), or primary current at no load, is given by the equation <math>\text{N}_1 i_1 - \text{N}_2 i_2 = \text{N}_1 i</math>. Then, writing <math>b</math> for the instantaneous value of the flux-density in the core, everywhere supposed to be the same, we arrive at the identity

{{block center|<math>e_1 i_1 = e_2 i_2 + (\text{R}_1 i_1{}^2 + \text{R}_2 i^2) + \text{S}\frac{db}{dt}(\text{N}_1 i_1 - \text{N}_2 i_2).</math>}}
